---
interface Props { tool: 'asm198x' | 'build198x' }
const { tool } = Astro.props;
const name = tool === 'asm198x' ? 'Asm198x' : 'Build198x';
const release = tool === 'asm198x' ? 'https://github.com/asm198x/asm198x/releases/latest' : 'https://github.com/build198x/build198x/releases/tag/build198x-v0.2.10';
const systems = [{id: 'macos', name: 'macOS'}, {id: 'windows', name: 'Windows'}, {id: 'linux', name: 'Linux'}];
---
<details class="tool-install">
  <summary>Install {name}</summary>
  <div data-setup-tabs>
    <div class="tab-bar" role="tablist" aria-label={`${name} operating system`}>
      {systems.map((os, index) => <button type="button" class="tab" role="tab" id={`${tool}-tab-${os.id}`} aria-controls={`${tool}-${os.id}`} aria-selected={index === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} tabindex={index === 0 ? 0 : -1}>{os.name}</button>)}
    </div>
    {systems.map(os => <div class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel" id={`${tool}-${os.id}`} aria-labelledby={`${tool}-tab-${os.id}`} tabindex="0">
      <h4 class="os-heading">{os.name}</h4>
      {os.id !== 'windows' ? <>
        <p>If you already use Homebrew, install {name} with:</p><pre><code>{`brew install ${tool}/tap/${tool}`}</code></pre>
        <p>Without Homebrew, use the {os.name} installer or archive on the <a href={release}>{name} release page</a>. {os.id === 'macos' ? 'Choose Apple silicon or Intel to match your Mac.' : 'Choose aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu for ARM64 or x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu for x86-64 Linux.'} Extract an archive into a folder you can find again.</p>
      </> : <p>Open the <a href={release}>{name} release page</a> and choose the Windows x86-64 ZIP (<code>x86_64-pc-windows-msvc</code>). Extract it into a folder you can find again.</p>}
      <p>The commands below use <code>{tool}</code> by name. {os.id === 'windows' ? <>Add the extracted folder to your user <code>Path</code> in Windows Environment Variables, then open a new PowerShell window. To run it from that folder without changing Path, use <code>.\{tool}.exe</code> instead.</> : <>Homebrew makes the command available automatically. If you used an archive, add the extracted folder to your shell’s <code>PATH</code>, or use the full path to the executable instead. From its folder, use <code>./{tool}</code>.</>}</p>
      <p>Check that your terminal can find it:</p><pre><code>{tool} --version</code></pre>
      <p class="step-note">If the command is not found, check the executable’s location. A version number confirms installation; the program you run next checks the whole workflow.</p>
    </div>)}
  </div>
</details>
<script src="../scripts/setup-tabs.ts"></script>
